WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.120
Good to have you on Planetary and Daily, episode 75 for June 1, 2026.

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:07.620
Some interesting findings to cover today.

00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:13.080
AI analysis of CT scans links healthier thymuses to longer life spans

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:14.980
and lower disease risk in adults.

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:20.260
Researchers at Mass General Brigham used AI to examine CT scans

00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:21.960
from tens of thousands of adults.

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:25.540
People with healthier thymuses showed longer survival

00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:29.560
and substantially lower risks of heart disease, cancer, and death.

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:34.460
This small immune organ, once viewed as largely inactive after childhood,

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:37.420
now appears tied to broader aging outcomes.

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:40.560
The finding adds a measurable imaging marker

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:43.920
to ongoing efforts tracking cellular aging biomarkers.

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:47.280
Next steps include testing whether thymus imaging

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:50.360
can refine risk models in longitudinal cohorts.

00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:54.060
The thymus imaging study from Mass General Brigham

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:58.080
adds a concrete non-invasive marker to aging biology research.

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:02.520
Healthier thymuses correlated with lower rates of heart disease, cancer,

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:06.000
and overall mortality across tens of thousands of adults,

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:11.080
moving beyond the long-held view that the organ becomes irrelevant after childhood.

00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:15.620
This finding sits within the tracked program on aging biomarkers

00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:20.760
and raises whether routine CT analysis could eventually stratify risk or guide

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:23.400
interventions that preserve thymic function.

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:28.700
What remains unclear is how modifiable the thymus remains in midlife

00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:33.360
and whether imaging changes track with other hallmarks of cellular aging.

00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:38.620
Which existing longevity cohorts could add thymus metrics to test these links?

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:42.900
Most people assume the thymus shrinks into irrelevance after puberty

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:45.600
and plays no further role in adult health.

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:49.660
In reality, the organ continues to export t-cells

00:01:49.660 --> 00:01:52.600
and support immune surveillance well into later life,

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:54.360
even if it reduced volume.

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:57.360
Right now, as you sit reading this,

00:01:57.560 --> 00:02:02.320
your thymus is still shaping the diversity of t-cells circulating through your blood and tissues.

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:07.240
AI analysis of CT scans from tens of thousands of adults

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:11.700
showed that individuals whose thymuses retained more tissue volume

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:16.660
lived longer and experienced markedly lower rates of heart disease and cancer.

00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:20.540
The memorable detail is that this single imaging feature

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:24.360
tracked with broad survival differences across a large population,

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:27.260
independent of obvious clinical markers.

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:32.960
One practical takeaway is to ask whether future routine scans could flag thymic health

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:36.620
the same way they already flag bone density or vascular plaque,

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:39.500
prompting earlier attention to immune resilience.

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:42.580
From internal immune organ imaging

00:02:42.580 --> 00:02:47.360
to another electrophysiological signature that distinguishes conscious states,

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:50.520
we turn next to a newly identified brain rhythm.

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:55.860
Neurosycology researchers identified a distinct mid-brain oscillation

00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:58.960
present only during waking hours and REM sleep.

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:01.920
The rhythm is absent in other sleep stages,

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:07.320
positioning it as a potential biophysiological signature for specific consciousness levels.

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:14.900
The work maps neural dynamics that distinguish alert and dreaming states from deep non-REM sleep.

00:03:15.460 --> 00:03:21.640
It supplies a new electrophysiological target for studies of brain aging and neurodegeneration.

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:26.700
Follow-up experiments will test whether the signature shifts with age or disease,

00:03:27.320 --> 00:03:29.480
while that rhythm helps map brain states.

00:03:29.980 --> 00:03:35.980
Another technology now lets researchers both record and control large neuronal populations at once.

00:03:36.940 --> 00:03:42.420
Nature methods describes neural pixels optoprobes that combine high density electrodes

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:46.200
with optical waveguives for blue and red light delivery.

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:52.440
The device's record spikes from large neuronal populations while activating or silencing

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:54.580
defined groups via optogenetics.

00:03:55.460 --> 00:03:59.740
Mouse brain tests showed clean separation of recording and stimulation sites.

00:04:00.500 --> 00:04:05.140
The single-shank design simplifies experiments that previously required separate tools.

00:04:05.980 --> 00:04:09.600
Expanded use in chronic preparations is the next technical step.

00:04:10.240 --> 00:04:14.480
From probing living brains, we move to mapping the proteins that build them,

00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:16.880
where scale has just increased dramatically.

00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:23.480
A new iteration of protein folding software has generated predicted structures for one billion proteins.

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:30.820
The expanded database supplies atomic level models for proteins previously lacking experimental data.

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:37.500
Researchers can query the set to identify folds linked to disease or to design new enzymes.

00:04:38.300 --> 00:04:43.600
The scale moves structural biology from individual targets toward proteome-wide analysis.

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:48.860
Integration with experimental validation pipelines is the immediate next phase.

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:54.840
With structures in hand, the next question is how cells use them to self-assemble tissues without

00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:55.960
external direction.

00:04:56.920 --> 00:05:02.800
A perspective in nature biotechnology frames cellular self-organization as a core mechanism

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:05.160
underlying multicellular life.

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:11.880
Stem cell-based models are proposed as tools to map how cells spontaneously form tissues

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:13.560
without external scaffolds.

00:05:14.220 --> 00:05:18.660
The framework connects developmental biology to regenerative engineering goals.

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:24.540
It highlights safety questions around partial reprogramming that remain open in the field.

00:05:25.340 --> 00:05:30.980
Ongoing model refinement will test whether these principles scale to organ level constructs.

00:05:31.100 --> 00:05:35.600
Refining which cells carry precise edits remains a practical bottleneck.

00:05:36.140 --> 00:05:38.600
A new selection method addresses that directly.

00:05:39.300 --> 00:05:46.480
Nature biotechnology reports a transient AND gate reporter system that selects human hematopoietic

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:49.100
stem cells carrying intended gene edits.

00:05:49.900 --> 00:05:55.240
The approach raises the fraction of correctly modified cells after homologous recombination.

00:05:55.580 --> 00:06:00.640
It reduces the burden of off-target or unedited cells in therapeutic preparations.

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:06.500
The method targets a key bottleneck in ex vivo gene editing for blood disorders.

00:06:07.340 --> 00:06:12.740
Clinical translation will depend on scaling the selection step without compromising cell viability.

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:18.940
New imaging and probe technologies continue to sharpen how we observe both the body's internal

00:06:18.940 --> 00:06:25.220
clocks and the universe beyond. Before we go, keep an eye on scaling efforts for the AND gate

00:06:25.220 --> 00:06:31.660
selection system in hematopoietic stem cell therapies. That covers today's science and health news.

